WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.870
We are almost ready to develop a

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.510
fast algorithm for finding the 2-Break
Distance.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.710
The only thing left for us to accomplish

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.220
it is to prove the 2-Break Distance
theorem.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:19.400
Let's consider an arbitrary transformation
of genome P into genome Q.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.630
We don't know what 2-breaks accomplish
this transformation.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.360
But what we know is that during

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.760
this transformation, the breakpoint graph
of P and Q

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.780
changes into the breakpoint graph of Q
with itself.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:42.510
Which means that the cycle number of P and
Q is changing into the

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:48.740
cycle number of Q with itself which in
turn is simply the number of blocks in Q.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:50.395
Therefore,

00:00:50.395 --> 00:00:55.850
whatever transformation of P and Q
accomplishes it,

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:01.240
the number of red-blue cycles increases by
the number of blocks

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:07.340
in the genomes P and Q minus the number of
cycles between P and Q.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.650
And therefore, the next question I will
ask,

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.870
how much each 2-break can contribute to
this increase

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.680
in the number of cycles?
Let's look into this.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.730
Well, there are different scenarios on
which two red

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:29.700
edges from the breakpoint graph to break
effect.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:35.150
Every 2-break removes two red edges.
Consider for instance the case

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:41.370
when these two red edges belong to the
same cycle in the break point graph.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.120
In this case shown on this slide two red
edges are removed,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.410
substituted by another two red edges, but
the cycle number doesn't change.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.060
Here's another case where the same two red
edges are

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.810
removed from a single cycle in the break
point graph.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.710
But the cycle number this time increases
by one.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:06.710
And now lets consider the case when two
removed red

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.900
edges belong to two different cycles.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.870
You can see that in this case doesn't
matter

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:16.940
which two red edges replace these removed
red edges.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.610
The cycle number doesn't change.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:24.320
Actually, the cycle number decreases by
one in this case.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.490
And therefore, each 2-break increases
the number of cycles by at most one.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.650
We almost proved this theorem.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.620
But actually, we haven't proved it yet.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.460
My previous slide was simply an invitation
for you to look at the picture.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.872
Let's give a little bit more formal proof.

00:02:39.872 --> 00:02:44.970
So a 2-break adds two new red edges,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:50.220
and thus creates at most two new cycles in
the breakpoint graph.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:57.040
On the other hand, it removes two red
edges, and thus destroys at least one old

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.220
cycle in the breakpoint graph.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:04.410
And therefore, the change in the number of
cycle is at most 2 minus 1

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:10.300
which is at most 1, and on the
other hand, if you

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:16.290
watched carefully my proof, then you noticed,
there is always a 2-break increasing the

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:22.620
number of cycles by 1, and here it is,
the central case here present.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.080
In example of such increase.
And now is the 2-break distance theorem.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.600
We saw that each 2-break increases the
number of cycles by at most 1.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.260
On the other hand, there exists a
2-break

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.330
increasing the number of cycles by exactly 1.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.680
On the other hand it is so that every

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.980
sorting by 2-break must increase the
number of cycles by

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:51.990
the number of blocks between P and Q,
minus the number of

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:57.200
cycles between P and Q, and therefore
the 2-break distance between

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.190
genomes P and Q is simply the number of blocks
between P and Q

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:04.790
minus the number of cycles within P and Q.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.880
And armed with the theorem, we actually
can compute

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.570
the 2-break distance between
circularized human and mouse chromosomes.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:21.650
We know that human and mouse genomes can
be decomposed into 280 synteny blocks.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:26.070
Each of these blocks will be larger than
half a million nucleotides in length.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:32.780
The breakpoint graph on this block if we
look at this actually contains 35 cycles.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:39.150
And therefore the 2-break distance
between human and mouse equals to 245.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:45.150
Remember this number because it will be
important in the next section.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:49.330
Now there are numerous 245 step scenarios,
and we do

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:53.120
not claim that we know which one of them
is correct.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.705
Moreover, the true scenario may have more
than 245 steps.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:04.150
Important thing for us is to remember that
there are at least 245 steps

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.540
to proceed to our next section,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.220
where we will analyse the random breakage
model.

